# Querying & Mutating the Board

Three primary access modes following the [agent-facing-api](https://github.com/relux-works/skill-agent-facing-api) pattern, plus CLI for humans:

## Concrete Assignment Default

When a prompt already supplies a Task/Bug ID and task inputs, query only fields
needed for the next decision:

```bash
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260211-abc) { status checklist outcomeResources }'
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260211-abc) { status blockedBy derivedBlockedBy }'
```

Do not add routine `summary()`, `plan()`, `schema()`, or `{ full }` orientation.
Use `schema(operation=NAME)` or `schema(mutation=NAME)` only after an unknown
call. Board-wide observability and plans belong to an orchestrator routing
decision; full reads remain explicit audit/debug surfaces.

## DSL Queries — primary read approach for agents

Compact queries that return structured output. Zero session overhead (no MCP handshake), batch multiple queries in a single call. **This is what agents should use for all structured reads.**

**Output format:** Use `--format compact` for token-efficient output (~40% fewer tokens). Default is `--format json`.

```bash
# Board overview
task-board q --format compact 'summary()'

# Whole-project readiness with the existing summary folded in
task-board q --format compact 'summary() { observability-project }'

# Scoped readiness at each board level
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=epic) { observability-epic }'
task-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260211-def) { observability-story }'
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { observability-task }'

# Single element lookup
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260211-abc) { status assignee }'

# Bounded activity history (continue with cursor=<next_cursor>)
task-board q --format compact \
  'activity(TASK-260211-abc, limit=25, order=descending, kinds="progress,content", view=effective)'

# Filtered listing with field preset
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'

# Agent monitoring
task-board q --format compact 'agents(stale=60)'

# Batch: multiple queries in one call (semicolon-separated)
task-board q --format compact 'get(T1) { status }; get(T2) { status }; get(T3) { status }'

# Zero-argument and parameterized operations can share a batch
task-board q --format compact 'project_config(); models(agent=codex)'

# Execution plan
task-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'

# Schema introspection — discover available fields, presets, operations, and mutations
task-board q 'schema()'
task-board q 'schema(operation=project_config)'
task-board q 'schema(mutation=set_status)'

# Target-only spawn policy (explicit opt-in in every profile)
task-board q --format compact 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=developer, agent=codex)'
```

**Operations:** `get(ID)`, `activity(ID, cursor?, limit?, order?, kinds?, view?)`, `list(filters...)`, `count(filters...)`, `summary()`, `plan(scope?, mode=?, active=?)`, `agents(stale=N?)`, `models(agent?)`, `project_config(view?, role?, agent?)`, `whoami()`, `schema(operation?|mutation?)`
**Field projection:** `{ id status assignee }`; base presets: `minimal`, `default`, `overview`, `full`, `progress`, `planning`; observability presets: `observability-project`, `observability-epic`, `observability-story`, `observability-task`
**Batching:** semicolons between queries. Statements and results preserve source order. JSON batches return an ordered top-level array; compact batches format each result normally and separate adjacent results with one blank line. A single query remains unwrapped. Syntax and unknown-operation errors reject the whole batch before execution instead of returning partial output; handler errors remain ordered in-band error results and later statements continue. Element mutations in one batch run inside one activity transaction: when any statement is refused, the refused statements keep their own errors, every statement that would otherwise have succeeded is reported as `ok:false` with code `batch_rolled_back`, and nothing is persisted. A clean batch commits every statement and records one activity event per affected element, so a batch may change an element's checklist only once; issue further checklist changes as separate commands. Local and remote mutation commands both print that complete envelope before returning a nonzero exit, so the envelope always describes exactly what was persisted.
**Format:** `--format compact` (agent default) or `--format json` (TUI/programmatic)

**First-time setup:** Under the full profile, `schema()` returns the complete API contract. Under lite, skip the proactive full read and request only the failed/unknown call with `schema(operation=NAME)` or `schema(mutation=NAME)`. Parser errors for a known call include that same typed signature directly.

`activity()` is deliberately separate from element projections: ordinary
`get`/`list` reads never embed history. Its `limit` defaults to 50 and is bounded
to 1..100. `order` accepts `ascending` or `descending`, `view` accepts `raw` or
`effective`, and `kinds` accepts a comma-separated operation filter. Reuse only
the opaque `next_cursor` returned by the previous page and keep the same order;
the cursor is direction-bound and sequence-anchored for stable traversal across
concurrent appends. The local CLI and remote `/q` endpoint share this contract.
Activity is automatic infrastructure output; callers must not append routine
history themselves. An explicit `legacy_pre_feature` boundary means older
events are unavailable, not that they should be inferred. Deletion retains the
terminal typed history. Operators correct a bad record only with confirmed,
append-only `correct_activity`; the original remains in `view=raw`, while
`view=effective` applies the correction, and remote correction requires Admin.
Never replace bounded pages with `get(...){full}` or an unbounded filesystem
read.

`kinds` also selects the three DERIVED control-plane planes. `run` returns the
spawn run lifecycle mirrored onto the run's task, `change_request` returns the
Change Request transitions mirrored onto the element the CR is attached to, and
`workspace` returns the Story workspace operations mirrored onto the Story:

```bash
task-board q --format compact 'activity(TASK-260211-abc, kinds="run,change_request", limit=25)'
task-board q --format compact 'activity(STORY-260211-abc, kinds=workspace, limit=25)'
```

These events carry one typed `ledger_event` change — domain, ledger id,
enumerated event token, sequence, revision, from/to state tokens, a UTC
instant, and typed `oid`/`ref`/`id`/`token` facts. They never carry a summary,
message or reason: each ledger keeps its own human-readable text and the mirror
cannot transport it. The default unfiltered view includes them alongside board
mutations, and filtering by a board kind such as `kinds=progress` returns
exactly what it returned before the planes existed.

The runtime emits them automatically at the moment each ledger row is written.
`record_ledger_event` exists so the local and remote transports share one
statement and one validator, and it refuses any free-text payload.

**It is not an agent-facing mutation.** `task-board m 'record_ledger_event(...)'`
is refused, on a local and a remote board alike, with a
`ledger_mirror_unauthorized` error: a mirror is DERIVED from a ledger row, so
one appended by hand asserts a control-plane fact that never happened. On a
local board the refusal is enforced twice over — once on the CLI mutation
surface and once in the shared activity layer, which admits only the runtime's
derived-mirror sink as the source entrypoint.

**Stated bound — board-server boards.** The prevention above is a property of
the CLI, not of the server. A mirror bound for a remote board is relayed over
the same `m` transport, authenticating as the same principal, through the same
handler entrypoint, as any other mutation that principal could send, so the
server cannot tell a relayed mirror from one a write-capable principal composed
by hand — and it admits both. What the remote plane holds instead is
attribution: every mirror records the authenticated principal that sent it, so
a hand-minted one is traceable to its author even though it is not prevented.
Preventing it would need per-record attestation the transport has no field for;
admin-gating the operation would break the ordinary agent's mirror, which is
the behaviour the remote plane exists to provide.

**Server capability is proven, never assumed.** Before sending a mirror to a
board-server the CLI reads `/v1/version` and requires the `ledger_mirror`
contract. A server that advertises none, or a different version, is refused
with a message naming what it advertised — a server that cannot persist a
mirror would refuse `record_ledger_event` as an unknown operation, which looks
exactly like a mirror that was never attempted. A `/version` read that FAILS is
refused too, and separately: a failed read is not an absence, and treating it
as one would silently drop mirrors a capable server would have accepted.

Run heartbeats are deliberately not mirrored, and run `progress` rows are
mirrored only when the project sets `validation.mirror_run_progress_rows=true`
— see `docs/activity-mutation-coverage.md` for those bounds and for what
`task-board validate` reports when a ledger row has no mirror.

### Scoped signatures

`schema(operation=NAME)` and `schema(mutation=NAME)` return one flat signature object: `kind`, `name`, a typed `signature`, description, parameters, and examples; mutations also report `destructive` and `idempotent`. They do not include unrelated fields, presets, operations, or mutation contracts. Provide exactly one scope. `schema()` without a scope retains the full legacy response.

Known-operation parser failures preserve the underlying typed parse error and append the relevant signature. This lets an agent repair a call without reading the full schema.

### Recovery examples from the Story binary

Captured at Story commit `13afc672f01a38d5748cca533f33524aec8080d9`.
From the repository root, build without installing:

```bash
(cd tools/board-cli && go build -o ../../.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board .)
```

The commands below use that binary and the authoritative board selected by
`TASK_BOARD_DIR`. IDs refer to this Story's board; substitute existing IDs on
another board. Output blocks quote the exact emitted text (errors are stderr).
Exit 1 is an expected refusal, not a passing command.

#### Unknown operation

An unknown operation lists the known query operations and points to `schema()` for discovery and `schema(operation=NAME)` for one signature.

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board q 'fetch()'
```

Output (exit 1):

```text
query error: parse error at 1:1: unknown operation "fetch" (got "fetch"); known operations: activity, agents, context, context_boundary, context_candidates, context_retention, contexts, count, get, list, models, plan, project_config, provider_limits, schema, summary, whoami; see schema() for the contract and schema(operation=NAME) for one signature
```

#### Statement grammar and bare positional arguments

The scoped signature includes `syntax`: the first bare positional value is the element/identifier argument, and subsequent arguments use `key=value`. Thus `get(TASK-260907-bg79el)` matches `get(id:string)`.

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board q 'schema(operation=get)'
```

Output (exit 0):

```text
{"description":"Get a single element by ID","examples":["get(TASK-XX) { overview }"],"kind":"operation","name":"get","parameters":[{"name":"id","type":"string","optional":false,"description":"Element ID"}],"signature":"get(id:string)","syntax":"OPERATION(element-or-identifier, key=value, key=\"quoted string\") { field preset }; statements separated by ';'; the first bare positional value is the element/identifier argument; remaining arguments use key=value (no operators or lists); string escapes: \\\" \\\\ \\n \\t"}
```

#### Unknown projection field

A misspelled projection field suggests `updated` and points to `schema()` for valid fields and presets.

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board q 'get(TASK-260907-bg79el) { updated_at }'
```

Output (exit 1):

```text
query error: parse error at 1:29: unknown field "updated_at" (got "updated_at"); did you mean `updated`?; see schema() for valid fields and preset names
```

#### Bare element ID

An element ID alone is refused; wrap it in `get(ID) { fields }`.

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board q 'TASK-260907-bg79el'
```

Output (exit 1):

```text
query error: an element ID is not an operation; use get(ID) { fields }
```

#### Keyword IDs on mutations

Every id-first mutation accepts `id=...` (bare or quoted) as well as the first positional argument. This includes `link`, `add_checklist_item`, and `set_status`; supplying both forms is rejected. These successful dry runs preview without persisting changes.

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board m --dry-run 'link(id=TASK-260907-bg79el, blocked_by=TASK-260211-1saw6h)'
```

Output (exit 0):

```text
{"ok":true,"result":{"blocked_by":"TASK-260211-1saw6h","dry_run":true,"id":"TASK-260907-bg79el"}}
```

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board m --dry-run 'add_checklist_item(id=TASK-260907-bg79el, text="Verify recovery guidance")'
```

Output (exit 0):

```text
{"ok":true,"result":{"dry_run":true,"id":"TASK-260907-bg79el","text":"Verify recovery guidance"}}
```

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board m --dry-run 'set_status(id=TASK-260907-bg79el, status=development)'
```

Output (exit 0):

```text
{"ok":true,"result":{"dry_run":true,"id":"TASK-260907-bg79el","status":"development"}}
```

### `project_config()` runtime shape

`project_config()` is always resolved by the local CLI, including when normal
board reads and writes target a remote server. Its current top-level keys are
`configured_mode`, `effective_mode`, `board_source`,
`explicit_remote_override`, `project_root`, `config_path`, `config_exists`,
`local`, `remote`, `whoami`, `goals`, `spawn`, `codex`, and `version_control`.

The no-argument shape remains the role-optional general config surface in every
profile, including the full default. For a spawn decision, opt in explicitly:

```bash
task-board q --format compact 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=developer, agent=codex)'
```

`role` is required. `agent` is optional and otherwise follows the same
preferred-provider/schedule/runtime-affinity resolver as `task-board spawn`.
The narrow result contains `enabled`, `max_parallel`, a small effective
agent-context profile/source header, the effective allowed and target providers,
the active schedule window, canonical role, merged role ceiling for the target
provider, provider selection mode, and launch-composition enabled/status. It
excludes local/remote auth, `whoami`, Codex session, nested agent-context policy,
project paths, all unrelated provider ceilings, and version-control fields.
Active schedule allow-sets and provider refusals use the same fail-closed
resolver as spawn.

For Codex, `resolved_role_ceiling` includes the resolved boolean `fast_mode`,
`fast_mode_source` (`default` or `configured`), and `provenance.fast_mode` with
the exact root or remote key. Durable spawn status and JSON run state carry
`service_tier`, `fast_mode`, `fast_mode_source`, and `fast_mode_config_key`;
provider-limit retries update them to describe the provider currently being
launched, while each `provider_attempts[]` entry preserves the tier evidence
carried by that historical launch.

The lite profile's resolved `queries.project_config=spawn-preflight` setting
guides agents toward the target-specific call; it does not rewrite a bare
`project_config()` invocation. Supplying a view or target argument opts into
the narrow projection, whose role remains mandatory.

The policy members relevant to spawning and status mutation are:

| Path | Shape |
|---|---|
| `spawn.preferred_agent` | Legacy configured provider string, or `null`. It is retained only as compatibility evidence. |
| `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` | Currently effective `{mode, allowed_providers, source, config_key}`, or `null`. During an active schedule window, `source` is `schedule`. |
| `spawn.schedule` | `{configured, timezone, config_key, windows, active_window, base_preferred_agentic_system}`. `timezone` is always `UTC`; every projected window also carries its normalized bounds, key, and provider-system projection. |
| `spawn.launch_composition` | `{enabled, config_key, contract, schema_version, degradation}` without invoking `agents-infra`. |
| `spawn.ceilings` | Always reports effective `contract_version`, `authority`, `configured`, and `config_path`. V3 providers expose normalized `allowed_models` or `entries`, nullable `resolution_model`, confirmation mode, effort direction, merged role-effective policy, and exact provenance. Every contract version and input form reports `admitted_pairs`, the canonical admitted `(model, effort)` set with its digest — the field to read when deciding what a policy admits, since `allowed_models` is `null` under the entries form. An entries policy also reports `entries_expanded`, `entries_expansion_pinned`, and any unreachable/redundant entry `warnings`. V2 exposes `snapshot_version`, `snapshot_allowed_models`, `migration_required`, stable actionable warnings, and exact read-only v3 `migration` objects. |
| `spawn.selection` | Required flags plus provider effort vocabularies and supported model criteria. |
| `spawn.execution` / `spawn.tracking` | Executable background mode and durable run-observation contract. |
| `goals` | Static compact `board-goal-v2` capability with required Session Manager native reflection and the one-release `primary-goal-v1` / `board-goal-v1` adapters. The legacy `goals.primary` descriptor remains nested alongside the v2 fields for the compatibility release. It never embeds the current objective and is omitted from `view=spawn-preflight`. |
| `version_control` | `{configured, confirm, desired_commit_time, enforced_types, owner_message, acknowledgement_parameter, acknowledgement_value, config_key}`. |

Local mode uses `spawn.*` keys. Remote mode uses `remote.spawn.*` when that
object is present and non-null; otherwise it falls back to the whole root
`spawn` object. The reported `spawn.config_key_prefix` and nested `config_key`
values always identify the effective source. `preferred_agentic_system` is the
canonical config spelling; `preferred_agent_system` is not an alias.

`spawn-policy-v3` admission is exact membership in the effective
`allowed_models` set. `PolicyRank`, lifecycle, recommendation, registry order,
and price are not policy inputs. A v2 projection is compatibility evidence,
not an automatic migration: setup, query, spawn, and install never rewrite the
repository. For an explicit migration, copy the full projection's provider and
role `migration` objects under `contract_version: spawn-policy-v3`, review the
diff, then repeat full and target-role preflight queries. An effort-only v2
warning uses reason `spawn_policy_v2_effort_only_migration_required` and
explicitly says to omit `resolution_model` unless a fallback is intended.

### Project observability preset family

`summary() { observability-project }` returns the whole-project view. The
element presets work with the existing `get` and `list` operations; the task
preset also applies to Bugs because Tasks and Bugs share the leaf estimate
contract.

Every preset exposes the same nested `observability` field:

- `scope` identifies the project or selected Epic/Story/Task/Bug.
- `summary` carries the existing `byType`, `active`, and `blocked` summary,
  scoped to that element and its descendants.
- `readiness.estimateKind` carries the leaf or calculated
  Story/Epic/project projection state without dumping every missing leaf.
- `readiness.coverage` always reports `estimated`, `unestimated`, `total`,
  `ratio`, and `complete`, including fully estimated scopes.
- `readiness.rolledEstimate` is the known Fibonacci sum even when coverage is
  partial. Query the separate `estimate` field on the incomplete Story/Epic to
  retrieve its exact `unestimatedTaskIds` drill-down list.
- `isBlocked` / `blockedElementIds` aggregate hard dependency state across the
  scope, while `isAdvisoryBlocked` / `advisoryBlockedElementIds` aggregate
  derived cross-container waiting. The nested summary retains blocker details.
- Only the project preset adds `primary_goal`. Its active form is
  `{active:true,id,revision,objective,updated_at}`; after a clear or before
  any creation it is `{active:false,latest_ref}` (where `latest_ref` can be
  `null`). Epic, Story, Task, and Bug observability deliberately do not repeat
  this board-wide parent intent or treat it as element acceptance.

Plain `summary()` keeps its legacy response shape for compatibility.

## DSL Mutations — structured writes for agents

Write operations through the same DSL engine. All mutations support `--dry-run` preview. Destructive mutations require `--confirm`.

Element-ID mutations accept the first positional argument or `id=ID` (bare or quoted), matching their schema signatures: `link(ID, blocked_by=OTHER)` and `link(id="ID", blocked_by=OTHER)` are equivalent. Supplying both forms is rejected. `create(type=..., parent=...)` retains its keyword syntax.

```bash
# Status changes
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-12, status=development)'

# Evidence-backed role handoff (preferred producer end transition)
task-board handoff TASK-12 --role developer

# Task/Bug estimate enum
task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-12, estimate=notEstimated)'
task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-12, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5)'

# Create elements
task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-05, name=new-task)'
task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-05, name=new-task, description="Implement feature", scope="Backend only", ac="Tests pass")'

# Update README fields
task-board m 'set_details(TASK-12, title="New title", description="Updated desc")'

# Rename overview name/slug while preserving ID, README, progress, children, and resources
task-board m 'rename(TASK-12, name=better-task-name)'

# Assignment
task-board m 'assign(TASK-12, agent=claude-opus)'
task-board m 'unassign(TASK-12)'

# Dependencies
task-board m 'link(TASK-13, blocked_by=TASK-12)'
task-board m 'unlink(TASK-13, blocked_by=TASK-12)'

# Checklist
task-board m 'check_item(TASK-12, item=1)'
task-board m 'uncheck_item(TASK-12, item=1)'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-12, text="Write tests")'

# Notes
task-board m 'set_notes(TASK-12, text="Progress update")'        # append
task-board m 'set_notes(TASK-12, text="Replace all", set=true)'  # replace

# Move to new parent
task-board m 'set_parent(TASK-12, parent=STORY-03)'

# Resources
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-12, name="TASK-12_design.md", type=precondition, content="...", description="Design doc")'
task-board m 'update_resource(TASK-12, name="TASK-12_design.md", type=precondition, content="revised", description="Design doc v2")'
task-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-12, name="TASK-12_design.md")'

# Destructive — preview first, then confirm
task-board m --dry-run 'remove_checklist_item(TASK-12, item=2)'
task-board m --confirm 'remove_checklist_item(TASK-12, item=2)'
task-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-12)'
task-board m --confirm 'delete(TASK-12)'

# Batch mutations (semicolon-separated)
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-12, status=done); set_status(TASK-13, status=development)'
```

**Safety flags:**
- `--dry-run` — preview mutation without applying. Returns what would change
- `--confirm` — required for destructive mutations (`delete`, `delete_resource`)
- Mutation arguments are schema-checked. Unknown named arguments and extra positional fragments are rejected with typed errors before any write runs.
- Without `--confirm`, destructive mutations are rejected with an error

**Format:** `--format json` (default) or `--format compact`

**Introspection:** Run `task-board q 'schema(mutation=NAME)'` for one mutation signature, or full `schema()` for the entire contract.

`task-board handoff` resolves the role's configured end status, verifies all
checklist items plus an actual stored task-scoped outcome, and serializes that
decision with the ordinary status transition. The underlying shared `handoff`
mutation carries the already-resolved role/status for remote execution; use the
CLI facade so callers do not have to reproduce role-policy resolution.

The `estimate` q field is a nested enum. Task/Bug values are either
`{"kind":"notEstimated"}` or
`{"kind":"estimated","scale":{"kind":"fibonacci","number":5}}`.
Story/Epic values are calculated, never persisted or hand-set. Full coverage
returns the same `estimated` shape with the descendant sum. Partial coverage
returns
`{"kind":"partiallyEstimated","rolledEstimate":{"kind":"estimated","scale":{"kind":"fibonacci","number":5}},"notEstimatedCount":"1/3","unestimatedTaskIds":["TASK-..."]}`.
Story counts direct Task/Bug children; Epic rolls up descendant Task/Bug values.
New Task/Bug records must be estimated before entering `development`; legacy
records with no persisted Estimate section remain compatible and count as
unestimated in container projections.

## Grep — unstructured text search

When DSL can't help — searching for arbitrary text across board files.

```bash
# Find mentions of a term
task-board grep "AudioRecorder"

# Search in specific file type
task-board grep "blocked.*deployment" --file progress.md

# Case-insensitive search in descriptions
task-board grep "TODO" -i --file README.md

# With context lines
task-board grep "error" -C 2
```

## CLI — human-facing output and complex operations

CLI text output with ANSI colors is designed for humans. Use for operations not covered by DSL, or when human-readable output is needed.

When reporting board state to a person, reference each epic, story, task, or bug with both its concrete ID and its human-readable title/name. In practice, that usually means carrying both `id` and `name` in your query output, or using a preset such as `default` or `overview` that already includes them. ID-only references are not enough for chat summaries because readers should not have to resolve identifiers manually.

```bash
# Good human-facing summary inputs
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { id name status }'
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123) { overview }'
```

### Parent alignment in the unified goal ledger

Use the top-level `goal` group to maintain one provider-neutral, board-scoped
statement of the current `parent/primary` owner's complete objective. It
survives a parent-session replacement, records immutable `board-goal-v2`
revisions, and never changes a board element's status or acceptance evidence.
The command group is the one-release `primary-goal-v1` compatibility adapter.

```bash
# Create only when no primary goal is active.
task-board goal set-primary \
  --objective "Keep the parent goal aligned with the current user requirements." \
  --reason "First actionable requirement"

# CAS-replace the whole objective. Repeating the same objective is idempotent
# once the installed row already carries the routing-duty condition; on a board
# upgraded with a pre-fix goal it mints the forward-looking default instead.
task-board goal update --if-revision 1 \
  --objective "Keep the parent goal aligned with the current user requirements and active delivery work." \
  --reason "Requirement expanded"

# Optional: override the native evaluator condition the bound parent session
# is installed with. The default is the forward-looking routing-duty
# condition (every terminal run notice routed, no dispatchable leaf
# undispatched, or explicitly waiting on a human) — a condition that is
# still unmet at install time, unlike the retired "has handled this
# revision" acknowledgement. Non-empty, no colon; omitted on update keeps
# the active revision's condition.
task-board goal update --if-revision 2 \
  --objective "Keep the parent goal aligned with the current user requirements and active delivery work." \
  --provider-condition "Every review chain on this board has a filed verdict and no leaf is unassigned" \
  --reason "Board-specific remaining duty"

# Read current state, immutable audit history, or one immutable revision.
task-board goal get
task-board goal get --history
task-board goal get --goal-id PRIMARY-GOAL-260723-a1b2c3 --revision 1

# Clear only with the observed revision and an explicit disposition reason.
task-board goal clear --if-revision 2 --reason "Parent intent superseded"
```

`goal get` is read-only and remains available inside a spawned run. When
`TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is set, `set-primary`, `update`, and `clear` return the
typed `primary_goal_parent_context_required` failure with no side effects;
spawned owners must use `task-board spawn goal` or `task-board spawn goal
upsert` for their `run/RUN-*` owner. Both commands use `board-goal-v2`; owner
isolation prevents either compatibility adapter from silently updating or
clearing the other.

Every successful JSON response has `ok`, `board_scope`, `primary_goal`, and
`latest_ref`; mutations additionally carry `mutation`. Exact revision reads
add `primary_goal_revision`, and history reads add newest-first `history`
summaries. Typed failures use
`{ok:false,error:{code,message,current_ref,side_effects:false,remediation?}}`.
Remote primary-adapter mutations accept authoritative `goal=board-v2` or the
one-release `primary_goal=board-v1` compatibility capability. Direct v2
mutations require `goal=board-v2`, and reads never create a local shadow
ledger.

## Decision table

| Need | Tool | Example |
|------|------|---------|
| Discover full API contract | DSL | `task-board q 'schema()'` |
| Repair one operation/mutation call | DSL | `task-board q 'schema(operation=NAME)'` / `schema(mutation=NAME)` |
| Preflight one spawn target | DSL | `task-board q 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=ROLE, agent=PROVIDER)'` |
| Structured query (status, filter, lookup) | DSL query | `task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'` |
| Structured write (status, assign, link, create) | DSL mutation | `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-XX, status=development)'` |
| Evidence-backed producer handoff | CLI facade over shared mutation | `task-board handoff TASK-XX --role developer` |
| Board parent intent | CLI primary-goal group | `task-board goal get` / `task-board goal update --if-revision N ...` |
| Preview write without applying | DSL mutation | `task-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-XX)'` |
| Text search across elements | Grep | `task-board grep "AudioRecorder"` |
| **Phase planning (canonical API)** | **DSL query** | **`task-board q 'plan(EPIC-XX, mode=children)'`** |
| **Pretty-print / render / save a plan** | **CLI facade** | **`task-board plan EPIC-XX --render --format png`** |
| Resource management | DSL mutation / CLI | `task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-XX, name=TASK-XX_results.md, content="...", type=outcome)'` |

### Plan dependency diagnostics

The JSON `plan()` result includes `plan.projection_cycles` (an empty array when
none exist). Each entry contains container `members`, a closed `chain`, inducing
stored `links`, and projected `edges` with their `sources`. Compact output emits
one advisory line when projection cycles exist. **Compatibility:** the Go plan
model's `CycleNodes` now carries task/bug cycles only; consumers must use
`projection_cycles` for container cycles.

Cross-parent task/bug links derive story/epic edges at read time as
`derivedBlockedBy` / `derivedBlocks`; they do not write container links. Explicit
legacy container links remain stored as `blockedBy` / `blocks` and participate
in container ordering. Active derived waiting sets `isAdvisoryBlocked`; active
explicit blocking sets `isBlocked`. Task link/unlink and reparenting preserve
explicit container links.

`task-board validate` reports `PROJECTION_CYCLE_DETECTED` as an advisory warning
for container cycles (including those involving explicit legacy links),
`TASK_CYCLE_DETECTED` as an error for task/bug cycles, and
`UNSUPPORTED_CONTAINER_LINK` as a separate warning for stored container links
without task-level support. A task DAG can have a container projection cycle
and still produce a plan. `task-board repair-links` lists unsupported stored
container links; confirmation removes only selected links still unsupported at
write time, never derived edges or task-cycle links. See the
[storage and repair contract](file-formats.md#container-dependency-projection).

**IMPORTANT:** Canonical planning lives in `task-board q 'plan(...)'`. Use `task-board plan` only as a local pretty-print / render / save facade over that same planning model. Never manually create phase breakdowns or write `.planning/` files from scratch.
# Negative Evidence Contract

A passing suite means nothing unless something in it would have failed. This
file is the single source of truth for the rules that make a green check mean
something, and for the incident record behind each one. The operative wording
that reaches every spawned child lives in the spawn template section
`Evidence That Counts`; the reviewer's licence lives in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`;
the repeated-variant heuristic lives in `.roles/orchestrator/role.md`. Read this
file when you need the shapes, the failure vocabulary, or the incidents that
produced a rule — do not restate these rules in other documents.

| Rule | Operative statement | Evidence record |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Negative tests for gates | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Narrowing, not deleting | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Production call site | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Absence vs failure to read | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Prove, or report nothing | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Reviewer licence to defeat a gate | `.roles/reviewer/role.md` | this file |
| Many variants, one unowned decision | `.roles/orchestrator/role.md` | this file |

## Negative Tests For Gates

Any feature that GATES, REFUSES, VALIDATES, AUTHORIZES, or ATTESTS must ship
with negative tests that fail when the gate admits what it is supposed to
reject. A positive test proving the happy path passes is not evidence that the
gate works; it is evidence that the gate is reachable.

This is the dominant defect class in this repository's 2026-07-28..08-03 waves.
Every instance below was found because a reviewer deliberately attacked the
gate, not because anyone read the code:

- the fact-assay attested a COLD child that never inherited the context, because
  it checked inventory, session id, digests, and output shape, but never
  inheritance;
- the assay fork had repository tool access, so it could answer from disk while
  the base was false;
- the builder attestation accepted a correctly-shaped forged digest that the
  caller minted itself;
- clearance enforcement existed but no production path called it, and counting
  tests around a fake proved nothing;
- an unknown assay event preserved a false zero-tool observation;
- a provider capability was declared absent to disable a flaky check, and the
  claim did not reproduce (9/11 success).

## Standard Negative Shapes

Use these names in checklists, verdicts, and test names. They are the shapes a
negative test must cover and the shapes a reviewer looks for first.

| Shape | What it looks like when nobody tests for it | What the negative test must do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Forged or self-minted evidence | The caller supplies the proof that the gate checks, and a correctly-shaped value passes. | Mint the evidence in the test the way a caller could, and require refusal. |
| Absent evidence treated as satisfied | Missing input takes the permissive branch; the suite only ever supplies the input. | Remove the evidence entirely and require refusal, not a default-pass. |
| Check present but uncalled from production | The guard is implemented and unit-tested by direct call; no production path invokes it. | Drive the real entry point — launch, materialize, resolve, publish — and name the production call site. |
| Bypass path around the check | One entry point enforces; a second, older, or retry/renewal path does not. | Exercise every entry point that reaches the protected state, including retry, renewal, resume, and recovery, and every delivery surface that carries the rule — a contract clause reaching one context profile and not the other is this shape. |
| Capability claim that does not reproduce | A capability is declared absent or present to make a flaky check go away. | Re-run the claim enough times to state a real rate, or report unknown. |

## Derive Completeness From An Independent Source

Completeness is derived from the pinned source or generated artifact, never
enumerated by hand. When a registry, schema set, or declared scope defines the
members, derive the expected set from that source and fail when any member lacks
enforcement. This rule comes from five consecutive review rounds that each
closed exactly the hand-picked members named by the previous reviewer; the next
unlisted member defeated every list.

A completeness test that reads the implementation's own table is circular and
can never detect a missing entry: removing the entry also removes it from the
test's expected set. Derive the expectation from the normative source of truth,
not from the code under test. This distinction was what let the next Story's
round-1 review demand a source-derived completeness test instead of accepting
the producer's two selected cases.

## Prove Exact Bounds At The Production Entry

Prove every bound in both directions at the production entry: accept at the
limit and refuse past the limit. Character bounds count characters rather than
bytes. The motivating failure was a value containing 3,999 characters but
5,999 bytes that production rejected with a byte comparison even though the
normative `string[1..4096]` bound permitted it; helper-only or ASCII-only tests
could not expose that narrowing.

## Disable Every Refusal Clause

Disable each refusal clause individually. If the suite stays green, that clause
is a hole unless the test or evidence documents it as provably subsumed and
names the check that subsumes it. This is measured rather than hypothetical: in
one file, disabling 110 clause sites produced 45 surviving mutants, and 12 of
those survivors were genuine widenings.

Numeric-bound mutation and clause-disablement are different classes, and one
harness rarely generates both. Numeric mutation finds off-by-one defects but
cannot express disabling a tagged-union arm, cross-field equality, or grammar
predicate; clause disablement finds those omissions but cannot move a numeric
boundary. The 110-site clause run exposed widenings that the numeric harness
could not generate, so run both classes.

## Check Refusals Against The Normative Source

A green test can defend a bug when it asserts a refusal that the normative
source does not require. One observed test required production to reject values
that the pinned specification permitted, so the suite protected the narrowing
instead of detecting it. Whenever a test encodes a refusal, check that refusal
against the pinned source rather than copying current behaviour into the
assertion.

## Eliminate Ambient Test Inputs

No test may depend on ambient environment. Fixtures set explicit identity and
all other values that could otherwise come from global configuration, the OS,
or developer-machine state. A developer machine let Git derive an author name
from the OS and passed the suite, while a CI runner with an empty GECOS field
failed with `empty ident name`; only CI can catch this class, so the fixture
must make its identity deterministic before CI exercises it.

## Prove A Bound By Narrowing, Not By Deleting

A test that removes a gate entirely proves only that the gate exists. It says
nothing about whether the gate covers the right class. Restrict the gate to a
narrower condition instead and require a named test to fail.

Two repository incidents where a narrowed gate survived a green suite:

- **BUG-260801-3kiloe F1** — the recovery successor cap was proven only by total
  removal. Narrowing it to provider-limit signals left every other recoverable
  failure unbounded, with the whole suite still green.
- **TASK-260802-17wetr F16** — thirteen cycles later, the same shape: the
  probe-lease cap survived deletion tests while the renewal path escaped it.
- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 1** — this contract itself. The reviewer clauses
  landed in the role body and the shipped test checked the role file and the
  lite template separately, so both halves were green while the composed lite
  prompt carried neither. Assert on the composed production artifact, not on the
  pieces it is built from.
- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 2** — the same contract, one level up, and the
  clearest statement of the difference between the two shapes. The cycle-1 fix
  did assert on the composed prompt, but it called the composition helper
  directly and handed it the role, task-class filter, and profile itself.
  Production builds those inputs inside the spawn entry point, so a mutant
  narrowing only the real lite-reviewer routing just before composition left the
  test green while an actual lite reviewer lost its gate-defeat licence. A test
  that supplies the inputs is testing the helper, not the path. Obtain the
  artifact the way the consumer does — here, read the prompt file recorded on
  the run manifest after driving the entry point — so role resolution, the
  task-class filter, and profile resolution are all under test rather than
  assumed.

Three traps when running the mutants themselves, all hit while proving this file:

- **The mutant must be re-run with `-count=1`.** `go test` caches on package
  sources; a test that reads `.roles/*.md` or any file outside its package at
  runtime returns a stale cached PASS after that file is mutated. A cached green
  is not a surviving mutant, it is an unexecuted test.
- **A passing assertion may be satisfied by the wrong source.** Two mutants of
  the producer DoD entries survived because the phrase asserted also appears,
  differently worded, in the shared spawn template. Choose assertion wording
  that only the intended source can supply, then narrow that source and require
  the named test to fail.
- **Restore the mutant from a copy, not from git.** `git checkout <file>`
  restores from the index, which silently discards uncommitted work in that file
  — during this task it reverted the whole cycle-2 change, not just the mutant.
  Copy the file aside before mutating, restore from the copy, and verify the
  restoration by diffing against it before rerunning the gates.

## A Gate Publishes Its Coverage As A Ratio

Prose about what a gate checks cannot distinguish 7 of 7 from 3 of 7. Every
statement such a description makes is usually true — it is true of what the gate
can see, and silent about what it cannot. The longer and more careful the
description, the more thorough the gate looks.

Report the fraction of intended targets the gate actually reaches, and assert
that fraction so it reddens when a target drops out of reach. A target no mutant
can redden is not covered, however elaborate the structure around it.

Three defects of this exact shape, each found only once a ratio existed:

- an AST call graph that recorded an edge only for a direct identifier call
  covered 3 of 7 guarded functions while every pin passed; the package's real
  dispatch was a function-value table, and the blind spot was invisible from
  inside;
- a classifier keyed on prose that a sibling gate was actively rewriting silently
  dropped 8 rows, taking a suite from 107 executed subtests to 66, while its
  completeness check `count == 0` reported nothing, because four is not zero;
- an ownership gate that proved a symbol exists and a case is linked reported 48
  sections as owned; measured against the specification, those bindings
  discharged 2 of 394 normative clauses.

A non-empty check on a derived set is the recurring form. `len(x) > 0` answers a
different question from `len(x) == expected`, and only the second notices a
narrowing.

## A Blind Spot Is Not An Empty Set

"Nothing is there" and "nothing this checker can see" are different facts, and
only the second is a statement about the checker. Reporting the first when you
mean the second converts a limitation into a clean bill of health, and the reader
has no way to tell.

An ownership gate scanned for uppercase RFC 2119 keywords. Sections stating their
obligations in declarative present tense scored zero, were bucketed as carrying
no obligations, and were admitted with no evidence required. Its documentation
said "there is no obligation to enumerate". The true sentence was "the section
carries no keyword line, so the scanner cannot see its obligations" — and 19 of
151 headings were free-admission surface the moment they were bound, including a
19-row exit-code registry and a closed manifest specification.

Two rules follow:

- State the bound of what the checker can see, next to what it reports. A
  residual class that is named can be attacked later; one that is implied cannot.
- Do not let the bucket for "nothing to check" also be the bucket that skips the
  check. Where absence of measurable obligation is a legitimate state, require an
  explicit reviewed justification for it, so the weakest entries are the ones
  that must say the most rather than the least.

## An Absence And A Failure To Read Are Different Facts

Absence is a legitimate state with a defined fallback. A failed, partial, or
malformed read is evidence of a problem. Treating the second as the first lets
the fallback launder a fault into a normal-looking value, and a fallback defined
for absence must never fire on a read failure.

Each of these was a separate defect in this repository:

- a non-empty value read as verified;
- an unknown scanner version read as clean;
- a partial provenance record read as absent;
- a zero pid start time read as alive;
- a malformed `api_error_status` read as missing;
- an unparsed envelope read as no envelope — this one produced a false provider
  suppression that would have hidden a healthy provider for hours.

## Prove, Or Report Nothing

Where a property cannot be established, report unparsed or unknown rather than
inferring it from a proxy signal. A plausible guess is worse than an explicit
unknown, because callers act on it.

Incident: headless top-level inference in the Claude classifier was unsound in
principle and produced six variants before it was deleted rather than
strengthened.

## Many Variants, One Unowned Decision

A long run of same-shaped variants usually means a decision has more than one
owner, not that there are many bugs. Eleven framing variants stopped reproducing
only when structure got a single scanner and a single state type; every per-site
patch before that produced the next variant. The operative wording is in
`.roles/orchestrator/role.md`: unify the decision, do not patch each site.

## Reviewer Licence

Reviewers are explicitly licensed to attempt to defeat a gate and to reject a
change whose only evidence is positive-path. Grep for callers before believing a
guard is in force. The operative wording is in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`.

## Exact-Evidence Reuse Is Not Full Replay

A green validation result is evidence about ONE candidate: the source and test
tree it ran against, the suite and configuration it ran with, and the
environment it ran in. Progressive checks may reuse that exact result while
all four identities are unchanged; the moment any of them differs, the result
is evidence about a different candidate and a rerun is required. The runtime
binding — which identities are hashed, what `reused_from_revision` records,
and why an absent or malformed environment identity forces a reasoned rerun —
is [docs/delivery-validation.md](../docs/delivery-validation.md). This page
states the shapes a plan and a reviewer check against.

- **Freeze the candidate before execution.** Evidence is bound to a captured
  tree, never to "the worktree as it is now". A result produced while the tree
  was still moving is evidence about no candidate at all.
- **Full replay needs a reason.** Re-running the whole configured suite is
  justified by exactly three things: a changed input identity, coverage the
  reused evidence does not carry, or a recorded diagnostic reason. "To be
  safe", "the runbook always does", or a retry that lost track of what already
  ran are not reasons, and each burns the same budget the incident measured.
- **Reuse never disarms the reviewer.** An independent reviewer retains
  targeted adversarial discretion over a reused green: the attack described in
  `Licence To Defeat A Gate` is against the candidate, not against the log,
  and a reused result is one more input to attack, not a waiver.
- **References and digests replace duplicated corpora.** A manifest of
  content digests proves what was checked; a second copy of the corpus proves
  only that the bytes were attached again. Portable archives are for a named
  consumer that cannot resolve references, and for nothing else.

## Refusal Rows: Research And Validation Budgets

The rows below are what the guidance REFUSES, and the two controls are what it
must keep admitting. Check a research plan, a rework brief, or a validation
decision against both halves: a rule that refuses everything satisfies the
first half and fails the second. Each refusal row names the observed incident
behind it (`TASK-260909-2ax2l1` retrospective and addendum).

| # | Scenario | Verdict | Why, and what the incident looked like |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| R1 | An agent justifies a second serial research prerequisite by consistency alone — "the sibling leaf has a gate, so this class needs one too", "the harness should also cover", symmetry with a prior leaf | **Refused.** A second serial prerequisite needs a checked spec gap (`solution-architect` rule 3: the missing piece, the requirement left incomplete, the spec sections checked) and its own budget; otherwise the finding is a focused regression in the owning leaf | Three nested gate leaves (contract gate, atom shapes, codepoints) were spawned from `repeat-of:` verdicts; production coverage stayed 0/28 vectors, 0/32 guards |
| R2 | Reuse of a green result after the harness, the source tree, the test tree, the configuration or the environment changed | **Refused.** Any changed identity makes the evidence about a different candidate; rerun with the diagnostic reason recorded. An earlier empty candidate never exempts a later source change | Reviewer anchors on a stale producer/revision are documented in two verdicts; a candidate was reviewed against a tree it no longer was |
| R3 | A gate claimed green with logs missing, truncated, unreadable, or ending before the last command | **Refused.** A failed or partial read is not a pass and not an absence; report which commands ran, which exit codes were observed, and mark the rest unrun. A cached `ok` on a package that reads files outside its module is this row | A 20-command suite timed out at command 13 of 20 after twelve greens; the seven unrun commands are unrun, not passed |
| R4 | A retry, resume, recovery or republish that starts its budgets from zero | **Refused.** Worker time, artifact bytes and serial depth accumulate across every continuation of the same leaf; a continuation inherits the budget position it was cancelled at | 13 of 42 runs were cancelled continuations; the chain re-entered full publication validation on an empty-delta research handoff until an operator directive stopped it |
| C1 | An unchanged green candidate: the same frozen tree, suite, configuration and environment, presented again for a later check | **Admissible.** Reuse the exact evidence; do not replay. The reviewer may still attack the candidate | The positive control for R2 and R3: identical identities, readable digest-matching log |
| C2 | A legitimate research-only Change Request with an empty `repository_delta`, whose task-scoped outcomes are attached and whose decision is recorded | **Admissible.** Publish it for independent review, skip source validation as `docs/delivery-validation.md` records, and route to the first implementation slice | The positive control for R1 and R4: one bounded contract task is the intended shape; twelve consecutive empty revisions with no consuming slice is what R1 and R4 refuse |

# Resources

Resources are owned by the board element they are attached to. Use explicit
CRUD operations against that element ID. Local payloads are grouped under
`.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/`; remote board-server uses the equivalent
element-scoped filesystem or MinIO object-key prefix.

## Resource Types

| Type | Purpose | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| **precondition** | Inputs needed before work starts | Design docs, specs, instructions, API schemas |
| **outcome** | Results produced by work | Research findings, implementation notes, analysis |

## Commands

```bash
# Create a resource
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_design.md, type=precondition, content="...", description="Architecture design")'
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content="...", description="Research findings")'

# Replace an existing resource
task-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content="revised findings", description="Research findings v2")'
task-board resource update TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_results.md --name reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome

# Adopt an existing orphaned local payload without rewriting it
task-board resource adopt TASK-260218-abc revisions/2/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome --description "Recovered result"

# Delete a resource (destructive — requires --confirm)
task-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md)'
task-board resource delete TASK-260218-abc artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md --confirm

# View resources on an element
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { preconditionResources outcomeResources }'
```

## Element-Owned Storage

Resources from different tasks are not stored in one shared file pile. Each
element gets its own directory, and every resource attached to that element is
stored below it:

```
.task-board/.resources/
├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/
│   ├── findings/
│   │   └── investigation.md
│   ├── artifacts/
│   │   └── run-1/
│   │       └── output.json
│   ├── reports/
│   │   └── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md
│   └── revisions/
│       └── 2/
│           └── review.md
└── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/
    └── research.md         # outcome
```

The resource `name` is a safe forward-slash-relative path. Agents may choose
the hierarchy that fits the artifact and nest it further as needed. Every
segment must be non-empty printable UTF-8 and must not be `.`, `..`, an
absolute/drive-qualified path, or contain a backslash. Raw forward slashes are
the only hierarchy delimiter; malformed URL escapes and encoded or repeatedly
encoded separators/traversal are rejected. Attach related files to the same
owning element; do not invent a generic board-wide resource task or write
payloads into `.task-board/.resources/` manually.

Directories are structural nodes, not payloads. Resource CRUD, validation, and
handoff evidence recognize regular files only; a directory named like a report
cannot be adopted or used to satisfy outcome evidence.

Local and board-server filesystem element deletion is journaled and
crash-recoverable. Direct resource CRUD first resolves any pending staged
element delete under the board mutation lock. MinIO element/subtree deletion
currently fails before mutation with the typed
`ErrAtomicResourceDeleteUnsupported` capability error; ordinary resource CRUD
remains available.

References are tracked in `progress.md` as markdown links:
```markdown
## Precondition Resources
- [specs/auth/design.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/precondition/specs/auth/design.md) — Architecture design

## Outcome Resources
- [reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/outcome/reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md) — Research findings
```

## Spawn Integration

Precondition resources are automatically injected into agent prompts by `task-board spawn`. Full prompts preserve normal text inlining. Lite prompts use the resolved `agent_context.prompt.preconditions` policy: by default, text below `inline_bytes` is rendered inline, while larger text and binary resources are written read-only under `.temp/resources/` and represented by their name, description, byte count, SHA-256 digest, and local path. This means:

1. Attach instructions/specs as precondition resources to a task
2. Run `task-board spawn TASK-XX --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` — agent gets everything automatically
3. Agent saves results via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write, then `update_resource(...)` for revisions

## Best Practices

- **Use preconditions for agent instructions** — attach design docs, specs, or step-by-step instructions before spawning
- **Use outcomes for work products** — research findings, analysis, implementation notes
- **Attach to the owning element** — pass the Task, Bug, Story, or Epic ID that owns the artifact; the storage layer groups its payloads under that element directory automatically
- **Use explicit create/update/delete** — `add_resource` / `resource add` must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` when replacing an existing payload
- **Use adopt only for local repair** — `resource adopt` registers bytes already stored at `.resources/<elementID>/<relative-resource-path>` without rewriting them; a missing payload returns resource-not-found and an existing declaration returns resource-exists
- **Choose a useful hierarchy inside the owning element** — `findings/`, `artifacts/`, `reports/`, and `revisions/` are conventions, not a fixed schema; nest further when it makes the handoff easier to navigate
- **Use safe relative resource paths** — `/` separates validated path segments; empty, dot/traversal, absolute/drive-qualified, backslash, control-character, malformed escape, and encoded/repeatedly encoded separator or traversal forms are rejected in local and remote storage
- **Attach files, not folders** — directories only organize resource files and never satisfy resource existence or handoff evidence
- **Always use board commands** — never create/edit resource files or progress.md resource sections manually
- **Name handoff evidence with task scope** — keep the task ID in the resource path, for example `reports/TASK-260218-abc_architecture-design.md`, so `task-board handoff` can recognize it
- **Add descriptions** — the `description=` param adds a one-line description shown in spawn prompts

---

# Bugs

Bugs are elements of the board, living inside stories alongside tasks.

```bash
# Create bug
task-board m 'create(type=bug, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=crash-on-start, description="App crashes when starting recording")'

# Track like any element
task-board m 'set_status(BUG-260218-abc, status=development)'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Reproduce the issue")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Find root cause")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Write fix")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Add regression test")'
```
# Statuses

10 statuses organized in a workflow:

| Status | Meaning | Who sets |
|--------|---------|----------|
| **backlog** | Captured, no work yet | anyone |
| **analysis** | Researching, decomposing | anyone |
| **to-dev** | Ready for development | orchestrator |
| **development** | Writing code | sub-agent |
| **to-review** | Code ready for review | sub-agent |
| **reviewing** | Reviewer child is checking the work | reviewer |
| **integrating** | Accepted Change Request, not landed. Active, never terminal | `accept_cr` only; left only by the integration transaction |
| **done** | Reviewed and accepted leaf work, or computed container completion | integration transaction for Change Request work; reviewer verdict plus the acknowledged transition for inline work; parent aggregation |
| **closed** | Explicit administrative disposition: cancelled, obsolete, or won't do (reason in notes) | anyone |
| **blocked** | Concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision (full evidence packet in notes/resource) | anyone |

## Status Flow

**Happy path:**
```
backlog → analysis → to-dev → development → to-review → reviewing → integrating → done
```

`integrating` is on the path for work a Change Request owns — the repository
delta produced in a managed Story workspace. `accept_cr` is the only way in and
the integration transaction is the only way out; see step 4a below. Work done
inline, with no Change Request, has no `integrating` step and goes
`reviewing → done` on the reviewer's accepted verdict, with the commit
acknowledgement when `version_control.confirm` is on.

**Returns from reviewing:**
- `reviewing → analysis` — need more research
- `reviewing → to-dev` — code issues, needs rework

**Terminal from any status:**
- `any → closed` — won't do / cancelled
- `any → blocked` — concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision
- `blocked → backlog` (automatic) — the last live linked blocker reached `done`/`closed`; see *blocked vs is_blocked* below

**Note on closed:** Use `closed` for cancelled, obsolete, or won't-do items. Don't delete — close with a note explaining why. History is preserved. `handoff` refuses terminal elements; `set_status` refuses leaving `done` or `closed` with `TerminalStatusError` (`terminal_status`), naming the current terminal state. An identical `set_status` is idempotent.

For a leaf task or bug, `done` means its delivery was accepted and `closed`
means it was explicitly disposed without accepted delivery. A leaf may be
closed from any lifecycle state.

For a story or epic, `done` is computed completion: every direct child is
`done` or `closed`. `closed` is an explicit administrative disposition of the
container itself. A container may be closed only when every direct and
transitive descendant is already `done` or `closed`; an empty container also
satisfies this rule. Close or complete descendant work first, then close
containers from the inside out. `task-board validate` reports
`PARENT_STATUS_MISMATCH` for stored closed containers that still contain active
descendants.

Validation is strict by default for empty containers too: an empty Story/Epic
stored as `done` aggregates to `backlog` and is reported. A migrated local
board may explicitly set
`task-board.config.json -> validation.allow_legacy_empty_done_containers` to
`true` to suppress only that historical empty-`done` mismatch. Empty active
containers and every container with one or more children remain strict.

**Don't delete elements.** Use `closed` instead of `delete`. Deletion loses history and context. The only exception is removing test/junk data during board setup.

**Orchestrator review routing flow:** When a producer child sets `to-review`:
1. Orchestrator confirms the producer attached task-scoped outcome evidence.
2. Orchestrator spawns a reviewer child with explicit selection, for example `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`.
3. Reviewer child sets/keeps `reviewing`, reads artifacts/code, and writes the verdict.
4. If accepted → `done`. If changes are requested → `to-dev` (code rework) or `analysis` (needs more research), with notes/outcome evidence.
4a. When the reviewer was launched against a Change Request (a managed Story workspace run), acceptance is recorded by `accept_cr(ID, revision=N, evidence=...)` instead. That mutation writes `accepted` and `integrating` atomically: it sets the Change Request to `accepted`, stamps the reviewer run, binds the revision to its **producer role and archetype**, and routes the element to `integrating` — accepted but not landed, and accepted work never stays in `reviewing`. The Orchestrator routes a new tracked run carrying that exact producer role and archetype; that run — not the Orchestrator — runs `task-board worktree checkpoint <ID>` for a **non-final** leaf, which commits the accepted candidate onto the Story branch and leaves the leaf `integrating`. A Change Request with `repository_delta=empty` creates no commit and likewise stays `integrating`. `done` is produced only by the integration transaction, and `worktree checkpoint` or `worktree integrate` from a reviewer, an orchestrator, or any run whose role/archetype is not the bound producer's is refused. `accept_cr` accepts no `commit_ack` argument, its `evidence` must be a task-scoped outcome resource this reviewer run attached or updated (naming the producer's own `_results.md` is refused with `change_request_evidence_missing`), only the reviewer run that was handed the revision may call it (anything else is refused with `change_request_acceptance_unauthorized`), and the live task/reviewer checklist must be complete. All refusals precede CR, status, and activity writes.

4b. **`checkpoint` never closes anything.** A checkpoint makes no board transition at all: the leaf it commits stays `integrating`. When the leaf is the Story's last open child it is also its Story's integration unit, so `worktree checkpoint` refuses it with `change_request_final_leaf_checkpoint` and names `task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID>`, which lands the branch and is the only thing that makes the `done` transition. The refusal is a scope rule, not an acknowledgement rule: it applies whether or not `version_control` confirmation is enabled, and it leaves the branch, the accepted record and the board untouched so the integration path can still use them.

A checkpointed leaf is closed by its Story's integration, not by a later status change. `worktree integrate` lands the Story branch, which carries every checkpoint commit on it, so the same transaction that makes the final leaf `done` also closes every sibling that is `integrating` with durable checkpoint evidence — and only those. A sibling accepted but never checkpointed has nothing on the branch to land: it keeps blocking, and the fix is to checkpoint it, not to close it. That is also why a checkpointed sibling does not hide the Story's final leaf: the leaf that would close the scope is computed against the leaves this landing will carry.
5. On changes requested, the Orchestrator preserves the verdict, spawns the appropriate producer, and sends the next `to-review` handoff through a new reviewer. Repeat producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`.
6. If a child exits early or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, the Orchestrator inspects the run/outcomes and retries or reroutes through a focused child. Recoverable work is never handed to the human.
7. A `blocked` verdict stops routing only when it identifies a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision and records the full evidence packet; otherwise retry or reroute.

`reviewing` is a real state, not a pass-through. A reviewer child must do actual review work between the producer handoff and `done`; the orchestrator routes and monitors that review instead of performing the verdict inline.

`reviewing` is never a verdict, including under the Change Request flow. A reviewer run that ends at `reviewing` with no accepted Change Request is cancelled and a successor reviewer is spawned — that is what distinguishes "the reviewer decided nothing" from "the reviewer decided", and `accept_cr` does not change it. The accepted branch is derived from the Change Request record, not from the `reviewing` status.

`to-review` therefore means one thing: a producer handoff awaiting review. An accepted Change Request is `integrating`, never `to-review`; `task-board worktree status` and the Change Request state say how far that integration has come.

A tracked spawned producer cannot use direct `set_status(..., status=to-review)`
for its assigned element. That workflow guardrail returns a typed remediation to
run `task-board handoff ELEMENT --role ROLE`, which verifies checklist and
outcome evidence. Untracked operator status administration is unchanged.

`to-review` is an internal routing trigger, never a handoff of routine review to the human. Human input is reserved for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision.

Human-facing wording follows the board state. `to-review` means "ready for review" or "handed off to review"; it is not "done", "complete", "finished", "final", or "готово". Say a task is done only after the board item is actually `done`.

## Per-Element Review Policy

Every board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with
`create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with
`set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` query
projection. New elements and legacy elements without the field default to
`required`.

- `required` keeps the producer -> reviewer -> rework/reviewer loop. Only an
  accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.
- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but selection should favor a cheaper suitable effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates.
- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff from `to-review`
  directly to `done` only after checking acceptance criteria, checklist,
  validation results, and outcome evidence. Missing evidence returns to the
  producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.

This policy changes reviewer routing, not the need for board-owned evidence, and
never introduces routine human review.

## blocked vs is_blocked

Two different concepts:

- **blocked** (status) — explicit stop-the-line status for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Notes or an outcome resource must record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact human decision or external input needed to resume. Recoverable failures and ordinary rework are not `blocked`.
- **is_blocked** (computed) — from `blocked-by` dependencies; if dependency is not `done`/`closed`, element is blocked

A dependency link is a **fact** and stays recorded after the blocker is done.
The `blocked` **status** is a **condition**, and the board lifts it:

- A `blocked` element with at least one recorded `blocked_by` link is released
  to `backlog` automatically, inside the same mutation, when its last live
  linked blocker reaches `done` or `closed` — a direct `set_status`, a
  `handoff`, or an aggregate container transition. The link stays; the
  mutation result carries a `released` side effect with `released_by`, and the
  element's activity event (`operation: dependency`) records
  `status: blocked → backlog` plus `released_by: [<blocker>]`.
- Several linked blockers release only when **all** are `done`/`closed`.
- A `blocked` element with **no** `blocked_by` link is the stop-the-line state
  described above: it is never auto-released and never refused.
- An element that was already `blocked` with every recorded blocker terminal
  before a mutation ran is **stale**, not released — no transition in that
  mutation can be named as its releaser. `task-board validate` reports it as
  the warning `STALE_BLOCKED` (same severity as `UNSUPPORTED CONTAINER LINK`),
  naming the element and its blockers. Resolve it with an explicit
  `set_status`. A link whose target is missing from the board is unknown, not
  satisfied: it neither releases nor warns. `unlink` never changes a status.
  A container that carries an (unsupported) `blocked_by` link is released like
  a leaf, and deleting a done blocker leaves its dependent linkless — the
  stop-the-line state above, never warned.

CLI display:
```
TASK-05: to-dev [BLOCKED by TASK-02]     ← computed from dependency
TASK-07: blocked                          ← explicit status
```

## Automatic Parent Status Aggregation

After a child status mutation, the CLI recomputes every ancestor from its direct
children. It is derived state: an unassigned story or epic may follow an
assigned child without implying ownership of the container.

- If every child is `done` or `closed`, a non-closed parent becomes `done`.
  An already explicit `closed` parent stays `closed`; terminal child updates do
  not silently normalize it to `done`.
- Otherwise the parent adopts the highest-priority active child status:
  `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, then `backlog`.
  `blocked` children do not select a parent status; a parent with only blocked
  children keeps its current manual status.
- The recomputation can promote, escalate, reopen, or demote a parent, then
  continues through all ancestors. A parent that is `blocked` explicitly is not
  recomputed.

```bash
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=development)'
# TASK-260205-abc123 → development
# STORY-260205-def456 → development (auto-escalated)
# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → development (auto-escalated)
```

For Task/Bug records created with the current format, the first transition into
`development` also requires an explicit estimated value. Set it first with
`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The refusal
is a typed estimate-required error, parallel to the unfinished-dependency start
refusal. Records whose legacy `progress.md` has no Estimate section are
grandfathered and are not retroactively blocked; an idempotent status write for
an element already in `development` is not treated as a new start.

### Commit confirmation for accepted work

This gate exists so the commit-owning mover lays out its own delivered scope
as atomic, project-perspective commits and honestly acknowledges that scope is
committed — it is not a human checkpoint. An Orchestrator (or other
commit-owning mover) must not stop autonomous work to ask the human how to
split commits, or whether to acknowledge, on every commit or close. The mover
decides its own commit decomposition — how many commits, per-story or
feature-clustered, ordering, buildability — and commits that scope itself,
backdated per the project's commit-time policy when one is configured. Human
authorization is the standing grant to commit work, not a per-commit
approval.

This section is the single source for that contract. Role bodies, the spawn
templates, and the router carry only the ownership statement and a link back
here; they must not restate the mechanics below.

Top-level `task-board.config.json -> version_control.confirm` defaults to
`false`. When it is `true`, `desired_commit_time` is required. Any mutation
that would move a Bug or Story to `done` must then include the exact mover-local
acknowledgement `commit_ack=scope_committed`. This covers a direct Bug/Story
transition and a Task/Bug terminal transition that automatically promotes its
parent Story.

```json
{
  "version_control": {
    "confirm": true,
    "desired_commit_time": "evening MSK"
  }
}
```

```bash
task-board m 'set_status(STORY-260205-def456, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'
```

The error reports the transition-causing element, every enforced Bug/Story
transition, the owner message, and the configured desired time. The
acknowledgement covers only the mover's scope; foreign uncommitted work is
allowed and task-board performs no git clean-tree check. Never acknowledge
`commit_ack=scope_committed` when the mover's own scope is genuinely
uncommitted — commit it first, then acknowledge. Local evaluation and
status writes share one mutation lock. In remote mode an enforcing CLI first
requires the server's `version_control_confirmation=atomic-v1` capability, then
the server evaluates the acknowledgement and writes the child/ancestor statuses
under one serialized mutation.

A reviewer-archetype spawned run must not supply `commit_ack`, and must not
escalate the commit-or-acknowledge decision to the human. It records acceptance
evidence and hands it to the producer side — never to the human; every `done`
transition belongs there.
Which producer depends on the flow. An element under a Change Request routes
through `accept_cr` to `integrating` and is closed by the integration
transaction of step 4a, which supplies the acknowledgement itself. An inline
element with no Change Request keeps this gate: the producer that owns the
committed scope commits it and then makes the acknowledged transition.

Board structure decides how often this gate fires on an unfinished parent; the
rule that keeps it honest lives with the other decomposition rules in
[planning workflow](planning-workflow.md).

Structural reactivation follows one deterministic rule: adding a descendant creates it in
`backlog`, and adding or changing any descendant to an aggregating active status
runs ordinary aggregation through every ancestor. A terminal descendant cannot
be reactivated with `set_status` or `handoff`; create new work instead. Any `done` or `closed`
container on that path reopens to the highest-priority active aggregate status;
explicit closure is not sticky once descendant work becomes active. The target
is the aggregate child status, not a fixed `development` state:

```bash
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=to-dev)'
# Precondition: TASK-260205-abc123 is nonterminal.
# TASK-260205-abc123 → to-dev
# STORY-260205-def456 → to-dev (auto-reopened)
# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → to-dev (auto-reopened)
```

Automatic container transitions do not require an assignee. Hard dependencies
still gate automatic changes to `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, and
`done`: if a container has an unfinished blocker, its derived status is left
unchanged and the child mutation succeeds. Direct manual transitions use the
same dependency gate; they additionally require an assignee for `development`
and `to-review` — the two transitions that assert a specific worker is doing or
has done the work. `to-dev` is deliberately exempt: it asserts that the leaf is
queued and owned by nobody, so requiring an owner in order to record that would
be self-contradictory and would leave an unassigned blocked leaf with no route
back onto the developer queue.

`blocked` remains the existing non-aggregating exception: blocked children do
not select a parent status. Closed ancestors remain closed rather than being
silently normalized to `done`, so validation reports every affected container
that still has a blocked descendant. Resolve or close that descendant work; a direct terminal-status exit is refused.
For historical post-landing regressions, see the proven-landing
[`restore-done` route](tracked-background-spawn.md#integration-run-termination-and-historical-status-recovery).
An accepted candidate that already reached the protected trunk without
the integration transaction running — a revision accepted by an older binary,
or a non-final leaf landed by its own pull request — is closed by `task-board
worktree close-landed <ELEMENT-ID> --reason "..."`. The tool verifies the
evidence itself and records the method by name: `tree_carried` (the recorded
`candidate_tree_oid` is the tree of exactly one ancestor of the freshly fetched
protected ref that postdates the record's base commit; `landed_tree_not_on_trunk`,
`landed_tree_ambiguous`, `landed_commit_predates_base` otherwise),
`patch_contained` (the digest-verified patch resource reverse-applies to the
tip in a temporary worktree; `landed_patch_digest_mismatch`), or `pr_attested`
(`--landed-by-pr N`, the platform's merge commit on trunk — or, after a history
rewrite, exactly one ancestor with the same tree AND subject line, recorded as
`twin_of=<platform OID>` — and the element named; `landed_pr_not_on_trunk`,
`landed_pr_does_not_name_element`). A `task_delta`
closes only that leaf; a `story_final` closes the Story as integration would. A
zero-delta candidate needs its base on the protected ref and names no commit
(`zero_delta_base_not_on_trunk`); a CR-less integrating element is admitted as
`legacy_patch_contained` or `research_outcome_only` or refused
`landed_close_missing_evidence`; an element the route is not for is
`landed_close_not_applicable`, and an unreadable record or a self-contradicting
one is `landed_close_indeterminate`. `--dry-run` verifies and writes nothing.
See [the Change Request lifecycle](change-request-lifecycle.md#the-change-request-lifecycle-in-order),
item 9.

## Story Activation Reminders

An `agent_reminders` array is added to the JSON envelope of a production
mutation whenever that mutation's persisted changes include a Story
transitioning out of `backlog`. It is **additive advisory output**: it never
fetches Git, never mutates the board beyond what the mutation already did, and
never replaces the authoritative freshness gate documented in
[task-board config](task-board-config.md#trunk-discovery-authority-and-refusals) —
a reminder can render every authority field `null` and the mutation still
succeeds.

### Trigger

A change qualifies when all of the following hold:

- `element_id` starts with `STORY-` (case-insensitive).
- `field` is `status` and the change's recorded `old_value` is exactly
  `backlog`.
- The new status is `analysis`, `to-dev`, or `development`. Every other
  target status — `to-review`, `reviewing`, `blocked`, `done`, `closed`, or a
  `backlog → backlog` no-op — does not qualify, and neither does a
  reactivation from `done` or `closed` (only `backlog` is the qualifying old
  value).

Both the mutation's primary change and every side effect are scanned, so a
Task/Bug mutation that auto-escalates its containing Story from `backlog`
still emits the Story's reminder even though the Task/Bug itself never
qualifies on its own ID prefix.

### Deduplication and order

Within one mutation's full change list (primary change first, then side
effects in their recorded order), only the first qualifying change per Story
ID produces a reminder — a batch that touches the same Story's status twice
(for example a reopen side effect followed by the requested transition) still
emits exactly one reminder for it. Distinct qualifying Story IDs each get
their own reminder, in first-seen order.

### Payload shape

```json
{
  "code": "story_worktree_base_preflight_required",
  "timestamp": "2026-08-29T12:34:56.123456789Z",
  "message": "Resolve and refresh the configured integration branch upstream, compare exact OIDs, and record the selected Story worktree base before provisioning or trusting it.",
  "story_id": "STORY-260218-bbbbbb",
  "trunk_ref": "refs/heads/main",
  "trunk_oid": "8c3f1a9...",
  "upstream_ref": "refs/remotes/protected/main",
  "upstream_oid": "5e2b7d0...",
  "selected_base_oid": null
}
```

`code`, `message`, and the RFC3339Nano UTC `timestamp` are stable across every
reminder in one mutation (one clock read per mutation, not per Story).
`trunk_ref`, `trunk_oid`, `upstream_ref`, `upstream_oid`, and
`selected_base_oid` are always present as JSON keys and are explicit `null`,
never omitted, whenever that specific piece of evidence is not known. The
`agent_reminders` key itself is only added to the result when at least one
change qualified; a mutation with no qualifying activation adds no key at all
(not an empty array).

### Authority evidence: local versus remote

The five nullable fields come from a cache-only projection that is read after
the mutation persists and never fails the mutation on error:

- **Local CLI** — prefers a persisted workspace freshness record from a prior
  `task-board spawn` for that Story (`trunk_ref`/`trunk_oid` = the recorded
  integration branch and its local OID at spawn time, `upstream_ref`/
  `upstream_oid` = the recorded upstream ref/OID, `selected_base_oid` = the
  OID the workspace actually branched from). When no such record exists, it
  falls back to a cache-only Git discovery (`DiscoverCachedTrunk`) against the
  configured `integration_base_branch` — the same discovery order as the
  freshness gate, but it never fetches, so `upstream_oid` reflects whatever
  the last fetch already cached — and `selected_base_oid` is `null`, because
  a cache-only projection names no provisioned workspace. Any read failure
  (unreadable tracking config, unreadable ref) renders every field `null`
  rather than failing the mutation.
- **Remote (board-server)** — the reminder is produced by the identical shared
  `pkg/board/query` mutation code, so it fires on the same triggers with the
  same dedup, but the server wires no authority source (there is no local Git
  checkout to read), so every one of the five fields is always `null`; only
  `code`, `timestamp`, `message`, and `story_id` are populated.

### Mutation coverage

`agent_reminders` is decorated by one shared boundary that a subset of
production mutations route their persisted changes through:

| Covered — can emit a reminder | Not wired — structurally cannot emit |
| --- | --- |
| `set_status` | `normalize_legacy_status` |
| `handoff` | `set_estimate` |
| `create` (only via its ancestor-reopen side effects) | `set_details`, `rename` |
| `delete` | `check_item`, `uncheck_item`, `add_checklist_item` |
| `set_parent` | `set_notes` |
| `assign`, `unassign`, `link`, `unlink` (wired, but their own changes are never `status` changes, so they only ever forward an ancestor side effect) | context mutations |

`assign`/`unassign`/`link`/`unlink` are listed on the covered side because
they share the same decorator plumbing as `set_status`; they simply never
produce a qualifying change themselves. The "not wired" column takes no `cfg`
at all in `registerMutations`, so it cannot mint a reminder even if a caller
crafted a matching change shape.

## Landing a Story on trunk

This section extends the commit-confirmation contract above to the one path
where a `done` transition is produced by a command rather than typed by hand:
`task-board worktree integrate`, which lands a Story developed in a managed
Story workspace. The mechanics of the managed workspace itself live in
[tracked background spawn](tracked-background-spawn.md#story-worktree-isolation);
what belongs here is the ordering and the exact scope of the acknowledgement.

**Accepted comes before done, always.** A reviewer records acceptance with
`accept_cr`, which routes the element to `integrating` and binds the revision to
its producer role and archetype. No Git operation has happened at that point and
nothing is `done`. The Orchestrator then routes a new tracked run with that
producer binding; that run integrates, and the `done` transition is the *last*
board effect of a run that has already moved trunk. The superseded order — close the
board first, land the work afterwards — is withdrawn, because it produced an
acknowledgement that attested a scope no repository had yet seen.

```bash
task-board worktree integrate STORY-260205-def456 \
  --cr TASK-260205-abc123 --revision 3 \
  --commit-time 2026-08-06T20:01:00+03:00
```

**The landing gate defaults to refusing.** `integrate` requires the
`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` suite to be configured; with no
suite it refuses `validation_not_configured` before any ref moves. A project
with nothing to run states that explicitly as `{"commands": []}`. See
[task-board config](task-board-config.md#the-landing-gate-suite).

**Squash, fast-forward-only, and one explicit time.** The accepted Story
candidate lands as one squash commit on trunk; the branch's internal Task
checkpoints are discarded rather than replayed, and trunk is only ever
fast-forwarded. When `version_control.confirm` is `true`, `--commit-time` is
required, must parse as RFC3339, and is applied to the author date and the
committer date of both commits this command creates. The example above is this
repository's own policy expressed concretely: the previous day at 20:01 MSK,
that is `2026-08-06T20:01:00+03:00` for an integration run on 2026-08-07. With
confirmation disabled and no flag, the current time is used instead.

**What the acknowledgement asserts here, and what it does not.** The command
supplies `commit_ack=scope_committed` on the `done` transition itself, and that
transition is not reachable until the Story's squash commit is genuinely
contained in trunk — a resumed run whose trunk no longer carries the recorded
candidate refuses rather than stamping the acknowledgement. So the assertion is
exactly: *the repository scope of this Story is on trunk.* It is narrower than
a clean tree in three specific ways, and each of them is a normal, expected
state at the moment the acknowledgement is written:

- **The Story's own board delta is still uncommitted.** The board-state commit
  is created *after* the transition, precisely because the transition is what
  produces the board bytes it records.
- **Board paths outside this Story are untouched.** The second commit stages an
  explicit pathspec: this Story's own subtree, the resource directories of its
  elements, and the ancestor aggregation files on its chain. Everything else
  under `.task-board/` — `.task-board/.goals/**` and every other Story's
  subtree — stays exactly as it was: ordinary uncommitted working-tree state
  that this command neither examines nor stages.
- **Foreign uncommitted work is untouched**, on the same rule the section above
  states for a hand-typed acknowledgement.

**The two commits, in order.** First the squash commit carrying the accepted
candidate tree; then the `done` transition; then a second, board-only commit
titled `Record <STORY-ID> board state`. A Story whose accepted candidate has no
repository delta produces no first commit and still produces the second one —
its board record is the whole of its work product.

**Recovery is re-running the same command.** Integration keeps one durable
transaction record per Story, so a crashed or interrupted run resumes the
recorded phase instead of starting a second integration, and a completed one is
a no-op rather than a duplicate landing. A rollback is offered in exactly one
phase — nothing landed yet, trunk unmoved — and is requested explicitly with
`--rollback`; no later phase undoes a commit that is already on trunk. Partial
integration is therefore a state to resume, never a state to clean up by hand.

